; Mark one attribute cell as Willy's and kill him on a nasty
;
; If the cell holds the room's background attribute ($80A0) and C & $0F is
; not 0, the cell gets that attribute with ink 7 (Willy's colour; paper,
; bright and flash are kept). Cells with any other attribute (floor, wall,
; guardians) are left alone. Then, whatever the cell now holds, it is compared
; with the nasty tile attribute ($80BB); a match jumps to #R$90B6, which
; drops the return addresses of this routine and #R$95C8 and kills Willy.
; So the nasty test covers all six cells even where nothing is written
; (standing on a nasty floor kills him). Observed with test calls: a nasty
; attribute in the right cell of Willy's top row, or in the row below his
; feet with Y on a row boundary, jumps to $90B6; two columns to the right it
; does not.
; .
; HL Attribute buffer address of the cell
; C Low nibble 0: leave the cell alone (third row of cells while Willy is on a row boundary)
; O:HL Unchanged
@label=MarkWillyCell
c$961E LD A,($80A0)  ; background attribute
 $9621 CP (HL)       ;
 $9622 JR NZ,$962F   ; not empty: leave the cell
 $9624 LD A,C        ;
@isub=AND Y_CELL_MASK
 $9625 AND $0F       ;
 $9627 JR Z,$962F    ;
 $9629 LD A,($80A0)  ;
@isub=OR WHITE
 $962C OR $07        ; ink 7
 $962E LD (HL),A     ;
*$962F LD A,($80BB)  ; nasty tile attribute
 $9632 CP (HL)       ;
 $9633 JP Z,$90B6    ; Willy dies
 $9636 RET           ;
